Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedbool.shoddy — reckoner seed: bool.shoddy's binary layer
Rem
Rem HEX, BIN, INBASE, FROMBASE; BITAND, BITOR, BITXOR, BITNOT, SHL, SHR,
Rem SHRA, ROTL, ROTR; MASK, GETFIELD, BITAT, POPCOUNT, LOWBIT, HIGHBIT,
Rem CARD, CARDWITH; SIGNED, UNSIGNED, SIGNEXTEND, NEGW, ADDW, SUBW, MULW,
Rem CARRIES, OVERFLOWS; GRAY, FROMGRAY, BCD, FROMBCD, PARITY, PARITYBIT,
Rem HAMMING; MINTERMS, IMPLICANTS, TABLE; and the six logic gates the
Rem engine core does not already own — NAND, NOR, XOR, XNOR, IMPLIES,
Rem IFF. RckSeedBool(st) is the whole of this file's public surface: fold
Rem it over a state and every one of them is in the dictionary.
Rem
Rem THE WIDEST SEED, because bool is the widest domain bridged: bases,
Rem bit operations, masks and gates are four families that share nothing
Rem but the value they work on, and a keyboard that has one of them and
Rem not the others is a keyboard that cannot finish the job.
Rem
Rem EVERY BOOL* WORD ABORTS ON A BAD DOMAIN AND NOTHING KEYABLE MAY.
Rem That is the whole work of this file. bool.shoddy is deliberately
Rem strict — it refuses a negative, a fraction, a value that does not fit
Rem its width, a width outside 1..53 — and every one of those refusals is
Rem an Error, which would take the mill down with it. So each word below
Rem asks the question bool would abort on FIRST, and answers a refusal
Rem instead. R4.1, applied to a machine that guards itself thoroughly and
Rem in the one way a reckoner cannot use.
Rem
Rem The guard helpers answer a REASON STRING, empty when there is
Rem nothing wrong. A Result would carry a payload none of them has: the
Rem question is only ever "may this proceed, and if not why not".
Rem
Rem THE WIDTH IS A SESSION MODE NOW, AND THAT IS WHAT UNBLOCKED THE
Rem REST. This file once stopped at twenty words, and four of the five
Rem families it left out were left out for reasons that have since
Rem dissolved. The load-bearing one was the width: the rotates, the
Rem arithmetic shift and the whole two's-complement family each want a
Rem width, and forty words each carrying one as an argument is not a
Rem bridge but a second dictionary. WIDTH — a core word beside FIX and
Rem DEG, validated once where it is set — is the answer, and the moment
Rem it existed the width stopped being something the keyboard supplies.
Rem
Rem WHAT THE OLD HEADER SAID, AND WHY IT NO LONGER DOES:
Rem
Rem   * "a stack has nowhere to put None()" — it has { }. LOWBIT and
Rem     HIGHBIT answer a LIST that is empty when no bit is set, which is
Rem     one cell always and so keeps UNDO's accounting, and composes with
Rem     LENGTH and FIRST. seed-regex's RXGROUPS already had this shape.
Rem
Rem   * "a product term has no Cell" — true, and it never reaches the
Rem     stack. MINTERMS, IMPLICANTS and TABLE answer bool's own STRING
Rem     renderings, and a string is a Cell.
Rem
Rem   * "BoolCard's glyphs are UTF-8 and file I/O is Latin-1" — so CARD
Rem     is built on BoolCardWith, which takes its glyphs, and picks ASCII.
Rem     CARDWITH is there for a session that knows its terminal.
Rem
Rem STILL NOT BRIDGED, and these are boundaries rather than a to-do list:
Rem
Rem   * AND, OR, NOT. The engine core owns all three as Boolean words
Rem     already. Bridging bool's would give two spellings for one idea,
Rem     which is the rule seed-math applied to Rad and Deg. The six gates
Rem     that ARE here are the ones the core lacks — and they have to be
Rem     here, because A USER WORD TAKES EXACTLY ONE CELL, so a keyboard
Rem     user cannot define a two-argument XOR for themselves.
Rem
Rem   * BoolBitList and BoolFromBitList. POPCOUNT, BITAT, LOWBIT, HIGHBIT
Rem     and CARD answer everything a keyboard asks about which bits are
Rem     set; a list of indices is a programming intermediate.
Rem
Rem   * BoolIsTautology, BoolEquiv, BoolRowOf and the rest that take a
Rem     [ List Of Boolean -- Boolean ]. A reckoner quotation is a token
Rem     list and not a typed function value, so bridging them means the
Rem     engine calling back into a user program with a typed argument.
Rem     That is a design and it is not this one. TABLE sidesteps it by
Rem     taking the MINTERMS and building the predicate in here, where
Rem     writing a function value is an ordinary thing to do.
Rem
Rem   * BoolMinimiseWith. MINTERMS covers the common case; the
Rem     with-don't-cares form needs a second minterm list, and three list
Rem     arguments at a prompt is where a bridge stops helping.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "bool.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- pulling several whole numbers at once ----------------------------
Rem RckWholeArg already refuses a negative and a fraction, which is two
Rem of bool's three domain rules; the third is the 53-bit ceiling, and it
Rem is checked here because a value above it is no longer exactly itself
Rem and every answer built from it would be plausible and wrong.

Def BlWhole(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckWholeArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(x)
            BlUnderCeiling(word, x)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def BlUnderCeiling(word As String, x As Number) As Result
    If x > 2 ^ BoolMaxWidth() - 1 Then
        Err(word & " needs a whole number below 2^" & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(x), 0)
    Else
        Ok(x)

Rem Deepest first, stopping at the first argument that is not a whole
Rem number — seed-math's MNums, over bool's stricter reader.
Def BlWholes(word As String, vs As List Of Cell) As Result
    BlWholesAt(word, vs, 1, { })

Def BlWholesAt(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, k As Number, acc As List Of Number) As Result
    If k > Length(vs) Then
        Ok(acc)
    Else
        BlWholesNext(word, vs, k, acc, BlWhole(word, Nth(vs, k)))

Def BlWholesNext(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, k As Number, acc As List Of Number, r As Result) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(x)
            BlWholesAt(word, vs, k + 1, Append(acc, x))
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Rem ---- the reason strings ------------------------------------------------
Rem Empty means "nothing wrong, go ahead". Each names the bound it is
Rem enforcing rather than only that something was out of range, because
Rem "1..53" is the fact a caller needs and "bad width" is not.

Def BlWidthWhy(word As String, a As Number, w As Number) As String
    If w < 1 Or w > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a width from 1 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(w)
    Else
        If a >= 2 ^ w Then
            word & " needs a value that fits " & Str(w) & " bits, and " & Str(a) & " does not"
        Else
            ""

Def BlBaseWhy(word As String, b As Number) As String
    If b < 2 Or b > 36 Then
        word & " needs a base from 2 to 36, got " & Str(b)
    Else
        ""

Def BlIdxWhy(word As String, k As Number) As String
    If k > BoolMaxWidth() - 1 Then
        word & " needs a bit index from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth() - 1) & ", got " & Str(k)
    Else
        ""

Def BlCountWhy(word As String, n As Number) As String
    If n > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a bit count from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(n)
    Else
        ""

Def BlFieldWhy(word As String, hi As Number, lo As Number) As String
    If BlIdxWhy(word, hi) <> "" Then
        BlIdxWhy(word, hi)
    Else
        If BlIdxWhy(word, lo) <> "" Then
            BlIdxWhy(word, lo)
        Else
            If hi < lo Then
                word & " needs a high bit at or above the low bit, got " & Str(hi) & " and " & Str(lo)
            Else
                ""

Def BlShiftWhy(word As String, n As Number) As String
    If n > BoolMaxWidth() Then
        word & " needs a shift from 0 to " & Str(BoolMaxWidth()) & ", got " & Str(n)
    Else
        ""

Rem EVERY WORD BELOW TESTS ITS REASON WITH If/Then/Else AND NEVER Ifte.
Rem Ifte is an ordinary call, so both of its arms are evaluated before it
Rem chooses between them — handing it a bool word as one arm would run
Rem exactly the abort the guard was written to prevent. If/Then/Else
Rem compiles to two quotations and one Ifte over them, so only the chosen
Rem arm runs. This is the single most dangerous line to "tidy up" in the
Rem file.

Rem ---- HEX, BIN ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Total once the domain is checked: every whole number below the ceiling
Rem has a rendering in every base.

Def BlSeedHex(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "HEX", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlText("HEX", First(vs), BoolHex)), "( n -- s )", "n written in hexadecimal, uppercase.")

Def BlSeedBin(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BIN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlText("BIN", First(vs), BoolBinS)), "( n -- s )", "n written in binary.")

Def BlText(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole(word, c)
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, n)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- INBASE, FROMBASE --------------------------------------------------
Rem The general pair. FROMBASE reads text, so a bad digit is a fact about
Rem the input rather than a bug: bool answers Result there and this turns
Rem that into a refusal carrying the position, in seed-csv's shape.

Def BlSeedInbase(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "INBASE", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlInbase(vs)), "( n base -- s )", "n written in the given base, 2 to 36, uppercase letters past 9.")

Def BlInbase(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("INBASE", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlInbaseAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("INBASE did not answer")

Def BlInbaseAt(n As Number, b As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlBaseWhy("INBASE", b)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutStr(BoolInBase(n, b)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedFrombase(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "FROMBASE", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlFrombase(vs)), "( s base -- n )", "The digit string s read in the given base; refuses a digit the base does not have, naming its position.")

Def BlFrombase(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("FROMBASE", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(s)
            BlFrombaseAt(s, Nth(vs, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Def BlFrombaseAt(s As String, c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("FROMBASE", c)
        Case Ok(b)
            BlFrombaseOn(s, b)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Def BlFrombaseOn(s As String, b As Number) As RckWork
    If BlBaseWhy("FROMBASE", b) <> "" Then
        RckWorkBad(BlBaseWhy("FROMBASE", b))
    Else
        BlFrombaseRead(BoolFromBase(s, b))

Def BlFrombaseRead(r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutNum(n) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(BlWhyAt("FROMBASE", w, at))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBASE did not answer")

Rem bool's own messages open with the BOOL* word that raised them, and at
Rem the keyboard the word is FROMBASE. So the prefix up to the first colon
Rem is REPLACED rather than repeated — "FROMBASE could not read it:
Rem BOOLFROMBASE: ..." names two words for one refusal and neither of them
Rem clearly. The rest of the message is bool's verbatim, because it is the
Rem only place that knows which of the three failures it was: a digit the
Rem base does not have, an empty string, or a string too long to read
Rem exactly. Err carries no tag to tell them apart by.
Def BlReword(word As String, why As String) As String
    Let k = Instr(why, ": ")
    If k = 0 Then
        word & ": " & why
    Else
        word & Mid(why, k, Len(why))

Def BlWhyAt(word As String, why As String, at As Number) As String
    If at = 0 Then
        BlReword(word, why)
    Else
        BlReword(word, why) & " at position " & Str(at)

Rem ---- BITAND, BITOR, BITXOR ---------------------------------------------
Rem The three width-free words, and the only three that need no bound
Rem beyond the domain itself.

Def BlSeedBitand(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITAND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITAND", vs, BoolBitAnd)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise AND. Needs no width.")

Def BlSeedBitor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITOR", vs, BoolBitOr)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise OR. Needs no width.")

Def BlSeedBitxor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITXOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("BITXOR", vs, BoolBitXor)), "( a b -- c )", "Bitwise XOR. Needs no width.")

Def BlBits2(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- BITNOT ------------------------------------------------------------
Rem Takes a width, because a complement without one has no answer.

Def BlSeedBitnot(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITNOT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitnot(vs)), "( a width -- c )", "Bitwise NOT within the given width, 1 to 53.")

Def BlBitnot(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("BITNOT", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlBitnotAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BITNOT did not answer")

Def BlBitnotAt(a As Number, w As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlWidthWhy("BITNOT", a, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolBitNot(a, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem ---- SHL, SHR ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem SHL needs an end for bits to fall off and SHR does not, which is
Rem bool's own rule carried to the keyboard: a logical right shift cannot
Rem produce a bit the input did not have, so there is no width for its
Rem answer to depend on.
Rem
Rem SHL TOOK ITS WIDTH AS A THIRD ARGUMENT AND NOW READS THE SESSION'S.
Rem That is a change to a word that already shipped, and it is a change
Rem to the ARITY and not only to where the number comes from: the line
Rem that used to be "255 1 32 SHL" is now "255 1 SHL", and at the default
Rem width both answer 510. It is done because SHL, SHRA, ROTL and ROTR
Rem are one family and three of them reading a mode while the fourth
Rem demands an argument is the surface nobody can remember. SHR is
Rem untouched, because a word that needs no width gains nothing from one.

Def BlSeedShl(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SHL", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWideShift("SHL", s, vs, BoolShl)), "( a n -- c )", "a shifted left n places within the session width; bits above it fall off.")

Def BlSeedShr(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SHR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlShr(vs)), "( a n -- c )", "a shifted right n places, zeros coming in at the top. Needs no width.")

Def BlShr(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("SHR", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlShrAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SHR did not answer")

Def BlShrAt(a As Number, n As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlShiftWhy("SHR", n)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolShr(a, n)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem ---- MASK, GETFIELD, BITAT, POPCOUNT -----------------------------------

Def BlSeedMask(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "MASK", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlMask(First(vs))), "( n -- m )", "A mask with the low n bits set, n from 0 to 53.")

Def BlMask(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("MASK", c)
        Case Ok(n)
            BlMaskAt(n)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("MASK did not answer")

Def BlMaskAt(n As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlCountWhy("MASK", n)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolMask(n)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedGetfield(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "GETFIELD", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlGetfield(vs)), "( a hi lo -- v )", "Bits hi down to lo of a, inclusive and 0-based, shifted down to zero.")

Def BlGetfield(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("GETFIELD", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlGetfieldAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), Nth(ns, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("GETFIELD did not answer")

Def BlGetfieldAt(a As Number, hi As Number, lo As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlFieldWhy("GETFIELD", hi, lo)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolGetField(a, hi, lo)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedBitat(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BITAT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitat(vs)), "( a k -- flag )", "Whether bit k of a is set. Answers a BOOLEAN, never 1 or 0.")

Def BlBitat(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("BITAT", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlBitatAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BITAT did not answer")

Def BlBitatAt(a As Number, k As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlIdxWhy("BITAT", k)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutBool(BoolBitAt(a, k)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedPopcount(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "POPCOUNT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlPopcount(First(vs))), "( a -- n )", "How many of a's bits are set.")

Def BlPopcount(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("POPCOUNT", c)
        Case Ok(a)
            RckVals({ CutNum(BoolPopCount(a)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("POPCOUNT did not answer")

Rem ---- the six gates the core does not own -------------------------------
Rem RckBool2 reads both arguments as BOOLEANs and names the kind it got
Rem instead, so these are one line each. AND, OR and NOT are the engine
Rem core's and are not redefined here.

Def BlSeedNand(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "NAND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("NAND", vs, BoolNand)), "( p q -- r )", "NOT (p AND q).")

Def BlSeedNor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "NOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("NOR", vs, BoolNor)), "( p q -- r )", "NOT (p OR q).")

Def BlSeedXor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "XOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("XOR", vs, BoolXor)), "( p q -- r )", "True when exactly one of p and q is true.")

Def BlSeedXnor(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "XNOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("XNOR", vs, BoolXnor)), "( p q -- r )", "True when p and q agree.")

Def BlSeedImplies(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "IMPLIES", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("IMPLIES", vs, BoolImplies)), "( p q -- r )", "False on exactly one row: p true and q false.")

Def BlSeedIff(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "IFF", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckBool2("IFF", vs, BoolIff)), "( p q -- r )", "Material equivalence — the same answer as XNOR, under a logician's name.")

Rem ======================================================================
Rem THE WORDS THAT READ THE SESSION WIDTH
Rem
Rem RckPureOver hands the word the state as well as its arguments, which
Rem is the whole mechanism: the width is a session mode beside FIX and
Rem DEG, so a word reads it rather than being handed it. The alternative
Rem was a width argument on each of fourteen words, and fourteen
Rem repetitions of a number nobody changes between one line and the next
Rem is what stopped these being bridged the first time.
Rem
Rem THE WIDTH IS ALREADY GOOD BY THE TIME IT ARRIVES. WIDTH validates
Rem 1..53 where it is set, so the width arm of BlWidthWhy cannot fire
Rem from any word below; what can still fail is the VALUE not fitting,
Rem and that is what these guards are asking.
Rem
Rem Declared PURE, and they are: reading a mode is not touching the
Rem world, exactly as SIN reading the angle mode is not.
Rem ======================================================================

Rem One number, at the session width.
Def BlWide1(word As String, st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole(word, First(vs))
        Case Ok(a)
            BlWide1At(word, a, RckWidth(st), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlWide1At(word As String, a As Number, w As Number, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Let why = BlWidthWhy(word, a, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, a, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem Two numbers, both of which must fit, answering a number.
Def BlWide2(word As String, st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlWide2At(word, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), RckWidth(st), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlWide2At(word As String, a As Number, b As Number, w As Number, f As [ Number Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Let why = BlBothFitWhy(word, a, b, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, a, b, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem The same pair, answering a BOOLEAN. CARRIES and OVERFLOWS are the
Rem reason wrapping arithmetic is worth having at all, and a flag is what
Rem they answer.
Def BlWide2Flag(word As String, st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number Number -- Boolean ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlWide2FlagAt(word, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), RckWidth(st), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlWide2FlagAt(word As String, a As Number, b As Number, w As Number, f As [ Number Number Number -- Boolean ]) As RckWork
    Let why = BlBothFitWhy(word, a, b, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutBool(Call(f, a, b, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlBothFitWhy(word As String, a As Number, b As Number, w As Number) As String
    If BlWidthWhy(word, a, w) <> "" Then
        BlWidthWhy(word, a, w)
    Else
        BlWidthWhy(word, b, w)

Rem A value and a shift or rotate count, at the session width. The count
Rem is bounded separately: a rotate takes it Mod width and so accepts any
Rem count, but bool refuses one above 53 and the refusal has to be asked
Rem for rather than run into.
Def BlWideShift(word As String, st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlWideShiftAt(word, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), RckWidth(st), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlWideShiftAt(word As String, a As Number, n As Number, w As Number, f As [ Number Number Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Let why = BlShiftOrFitWhy(word, a, n, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, a, n, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlShiftOrFitWhy(word As String, a As Number, n As Number, w As Number) As String
    If BlShiftWhy(word, n) <> "" Then
        BlShiftWhy(word, n)
    Else
        BlWidthWhy(word, a, w)

Rem ---- ROTL, ROTR, SHRA ---------------------------------------------------
Rem The three that complete the shift family. SHL and SHR are above and
Rem SHL now reads the width from here too, which is what makes all four
Rem read alike at a prompt.

Def BlSeedRotl(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "ROTL", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWideShift("ROTL", s, vs, BoolRotl)), "( a n -- b )", "a rotated left n places within the session width; bits leaving the top come back at the bottom.")

Def BlSeedRotr(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "ROTR", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWideShift("ROTR", s, vs, BoolRotr)), "( a n -- b )", "a rotated right n places within the session width; bits leaving the bottom come back at the top.")

Def BlSeedShra(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SHRA", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWideShift("SHRA", s, vs, BoolShrA)), "( a n -- b )", "Arithmetic right shift: the top bit is copied down rather than zeros coming in, so a negative pattern stays negative.")

Rem ---- the two's-complement family ----------------------------------------
Rem SIGNED reads a bit pattern as a signed value and UNSIGNED goes back.
Rem UNSIGNED IS THE ONE WORD HERE WHOSE INPUT MAY BE NEGATIVE, which is
Rem why it cannot use BlWhole: that reader refuses a negative, and a
Rem negative is exactly what this word is for.

Def BlSeedSigned(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SIGNED", 1, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide1("SIGNED", s, vs, BoolSigned)), "( a -- v )", "The bit pattern a read as a two's-complement signed value at the session width.")

Def BlSeedUnsigned(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "UNSIGNED", 1, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlUnsigned(s, vs)), "( v -- a )", "The signed value v as the bit pattern that holds it at the session width; v may be negative.")

Def BlUnsigned(st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlAnyWhole("UNSIGNED", First(vs))
        Case Ok(v)
            BlUnsignedAt(v, RckWidth(st))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("UNSIGNED did not answer")

Def BlUnsignedAt(v As Number, w As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlSignedFitWhy("UNSIGNED", v, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolUnsigned(v, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem A whole number of either sign, still under the exactness ceiling.
Def BlAnyWhole(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckNumArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(x)
            BlAnyWholeOf(word, x)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Rem The ceiling is checked on the MAGNITUDE and the answer carries the
Rem ORIGINAL back. BlUnderCeiling answers the value it was given, so
Rem handing it Abs(x) and returning that would quietly turn -1 into 1 —
Rem which is the one thing this reader exists to avoid.
Def BlAnyWholeOf(word As String, x As Number) As Result
    If x <> Floor(x) Then
        Err(word & " needs a whole number, got " & Str(x), 0)
    Else
        BlKeepSign(word, x, BlUnderCeiling(word, Abs(x)))

Def BlKeepSign(word As String, x As Number, r As Result) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(m)
            Ok(x)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def BlSignedFitWhy(word As String, v As Number, w As Number) As String
    If v < 0 - 2 ^ (w - 1) Or v > 2 ^ (w - 1) - 1 Then
        word & " needs a value from " & Str(0 - 2 ^ (w - 1)) & " to " & Str(2 ^ (w - 1) - 1) & " at " & Str(w) & " signed bits, and " & Str(v) & " is outside it"
    Else
        ""

Def BlSeedSignextend(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SIGNEXTEND", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlSignextend(s, vs)), "( a from -- b )", "The from-bit pattern a widened to the session width, its sign bit copied into the new bits.")

Def BlSignextend(st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes("SIGNEXTEND", vs)
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlSignextendAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), RckWidth(st))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SIGNEXTEND did not answer")

Def BlSignextendAt(a As Number, from As Number, w As Number) As RckWork
    Let why = BlWidenWhy(a, from, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolSignExtend(a, from, w)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem Three ways this one goes wrong and each names its own bound: a
Rem source width outside 1..53, a value that does not fit the source, and
Rem a session width narrower than the source — which is not widening at
Rem all and is the mistake worth catching by name.
Def BlWidenWhy(a As Number, from As Number, w As Number) As String
    If BlWidthWhy("SIGNEXTEND", a, from) <> "" Then
        BlWidthWhy("SIGNEXTEND", a, from)
    Else
        If from > w Then
            "SIGNEXTEND cannot widen from " & Str(from) & " bits to the session width of " & Str(w)
        Else
            ""

Def BlSeedNegw(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "NEGW", 1, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide1("NEGW", s, vs, BoolNeg)), "( a -- b )", "Two's-complement negation at the session width. Zero negates to zero.")

Def BlSeedAddw(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "ADDW", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide2("ADDW", s, vs, BoolAddW)), "( a b -- c )", "a plus b, wrapping at the session width the way a machine word does.")

Def BlSeedSubw(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "SUBW", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide2("SUBW", s, vs, BoolSubW)), "( a b -- c )", "a minus b, wrapping at the session width; below zero it comes back round the top.")

Def BlSeedMulw(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "MULW", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide2("MULW", s, vs, BoolMulW)), "( a b -- c )", "a times b, keeping only the low bits that fit the session width.")

Def BlSeedCarries(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "CARRIES", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide2Flag("CARRIES", s, vs, BoolCarries)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a plus b carries out of the session width — UNSIGNED overflow.")

Def BlSeedOverflows(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "OVERFLOWS", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlWide2Flag("OVERFLOWS", s, vs, BoolOverflows)), "( a b -- flag )", "Whether a plus b overflows as SIGNED values. A different question from CARRIES, with a different answer.")

Rem ---- CARD, CARDWITH -----------------------------------------------------
Rem A mask read as a row of holes. 4294901760 tells a reader nothing;
Rem sixteen filled followed by sixteen empty tells them at once, which is
Rem the case bool's own header makes.
Rem
Rem ASCII BY DEFAULT, and that is the whole of the old exclusion. BoolCard
Rem renders in round UTF-8 glyphs that become question marks the moment a
Rem card reaches a tape or a saved session, because file I/O here is
Rem Latin-1. BoolCardWith takes its glyphs as arguments precisely so a
Rem caller can pick ones that survive being written down, and CARD picks
Rem them. CARDWITH is for a session that knows its terminal.

Def BlSeedCard(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "CARD", 1, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlCard(s, vs)), "( a -- s )", "a as a row of session-width holes, " & Chr(34) & "#" & Chr(34) & " for a set bit and " & Chr(34) & "." & Chr(34) & " for a clear one. Plain ASCII, so it survives a tape.")

Def BlCard(st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("CARD", First(vs))
        Case Ok(a)
            BlCardAt("CARD", a, RckWidth(st), "#", ".")
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CARD did not answer")

Def BlCardAt(word As String, a As Number, w As Number, on As String, off As String) As RckWork
    Let why = BlWidthWhy(word, a, w)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutStr(BoolCardWith(a, w, on, off)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Def BlSeedCardwith(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "CARDWITH", 3, RckPureOver(Fn(s, vs) => BlCardwith(s, vs)), "( a on off -- s )", "CARD with your own two glyphs, for a terminal that can hold them. Anything outside ASCII will not survive being saved.")

Def BlCardwith(st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("CARDWITH", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(a)
            BlCardwithOn(a, RckWidth(st), Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CARDWITH did not answer")

Def BlCardwithOn(a As Number, w As Number, onc As Cell, offc As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg("CARDWITH", onc), RckTextArg("CARDWITH", offc))
        Case Pair(Ok(on), Ok(off))
            BlCardAt("CARDWITH", a, w, on, off)
        Case Pair(Err(w1, a1), r2)
            RckWorkBad(w1)
        Case Pair(r1, Err(w2, a2))
            RckWorkBad(w2)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CARDWITH did not answer")

Rem ---- LOWBIT, HIGHBIT ----------------------------------------------------
Rem ABSENCE IS AN EMPTY LIST, and that is the whole answer to the old
Rem exclusion. These two answer an Option and None() at zero is an
Rem ORDINARY answer — a word that refused on zero would contradict the
Rem contract bool itself draws. A stack has nowhere to put None(), but it
Rem has somewhere to put { }: one cell, holding a list that is empty.
Rem
Rem ONE CELL, ALWAYS. A word pushing a variable number of cells would
Rem break UNDO's accounting, which is the rule seed-regex's RXGROUPS
Rem already keeps for exactly this shape. So the answer is never "nothing
Rem at all" — it is always one LIST, and LENGTH of it is the question
Rem "was there a bit?" asked in the words the keyboard already has.

Def BlSeedLowbit(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "LOWBIT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitOf("LOWBIT", First(vs), BoolLowBit)), "( a -- list )", "The index of a's lowest set bit as a one-item LIST, or an empty LIST when no bit is set. LENGTH asks whether there was one.")

Def BlSeedHighbit(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "HIGHBIT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBitOf("HIGHBIT", First(vs), BoolHighBit)), "( a -- list )", "The index of a's highest set bit as a one-item LIST, or an empty LIST when no bit is set.")

Def BlBitOf(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ Number -- Option ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole(word, c)
        Case Ok(a)
            RckVals({ CutList(BlOptCells(Call(f, a))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlOptCells(o As Option) As List Of Cell
    Select Case o
        Case Some(k)
            { CutNum(k) }
        Case Else
            { }

Rem ---- the codes ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Gray, BCD, parity and Hamming. None of them wants a width: they are
Rem facts about a number's bits rather than about a machine word, which
Rem is why they are plain RckPure and not RckPureOver.

Def BlSeedGray(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "GRAY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlNum1("GRAY", First(vs), BoolGray)), "( n -- g )", "n in Gray code, where consecutive values differ in exactly one bit.")

Def BlSeedFromgray(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "FROMGRAY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlNum1("FROMGRAY", First(vs), BoolFromGray)), "( g -- n )", "The number a Gray code stands for; GRAY the other way round.")

Def BlSeedParitybit(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "PARITYBIT", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlNum1("PARITYBIT", First(vs), BoolParityBit)), "( a -- k )", "The even-parity bit to append to a: 1 when an odd number of bits is set, 0 when even.")

Def BlNum1(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole(word, c)
        Case Ok(a)
            RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, a)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlSeedParity(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "PARITY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlParity(First(vs))), "( a -- flag )", "Whether an odd number of a's bits are set. Answers a BOOLEAN, never 1 or 0.")

Def BlParity(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("PARITY", c)
        Case Ok(a)
            RckVals({ CutBool(BoolParity(a)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("PARITY did not answer")

Def BlSeedHamming(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "HAMMING", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBits2("HAMMING", vs, BoolHamming)), "( a b -- d )", "How many bit positions a and b differ in. Needs no width.")

Rem BCD packs one decimal digit per four bits, so thirteen digits is what
Rem 53 bits hold and the fourteenth is the refusal.
Def BlSeedBcd(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "BCD", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlBcd(First(vs))), "( n -- b )", "n packed as binary-coded decimal, one digit to each four bits. Up to thirteen digits.")

Def BlBcd(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("BCD", c)
        Case Ok(n)
            BlBcdAt(n)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BCD did not answer")

Def BlBcdAt(n As Number) As RckWork
    If n >= 10 ^ 13 Then
        RckWorkBad("BCD packs at most thirteen digits, and " & Str(n) & " has more")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutNum(BoolBcd(n)) })

Rem FROMBCD reads a pattern that may not be BCD at all — a nibble above 9
Rem is not a decimal digit — so bool answers Result and this turns it into
Rem a refusal, the same bridge shape FROMBASE uses.
Def BlSeedFrombcd(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "FROMBCD", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlFrombcd(First(vs))), "( b -- n )", "The number a BCD pattern stands for; refuses a nibble above 9, which is not a decimal digit.")

Def BlFrombcd(c As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWhole("FROMBCD", c)
        Case Ok(b)
            BlFrombcdRead(BoolFromBcd(b))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBCD did not answer")

Def BlFrombcdRead(r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutNum(n) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(BlWhyAt("FROMBCD", w, at))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("FROMBCD did not answer")

Rem ---- truth tables and minimisation, as text -----------------------------
Rem THE INTERMEDIATE NEVER TOUCHES THE STACK. A product term has no Cell
Rem and inventing one would be a design rather than a bridge — but bool
Rem already renders both structures as STRINGS, and a string is a Cell.
Rem So these three answer the rendering and the BoolTerm stays inside.
Rem
Rem The minterms arrive as a LIST of numbers, which is the input somebody
Rem actually has after filling in a table, and is the shape seed-matrix
Rem and seed-stats already use for a structured argument.
Rem
Rem n IS A VARIABLE COUNT AND NOT THE SESSION WIDTH. These are Boolean
Rem variables, not bits of a machine word, and letting WIDTH govern a
Rem truth table would be one mode quietly answering a question nobody
Rem pointed it at.

Def BlSeedMinterms(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "MINTERMS", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlTerms("MINTERMS", vs, BlSopOf)), "( ms n -- s )", "The minimised sum-of-products covering those minterms over n variables, as text. Minimal in practice rather than proven minimal.")

Def BlSeedImplicants(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "IMPLICANTS", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlTerms("IMPLICANTS", vs, BlPiOf)), "( ms n -- s )", "Every prime implicant of those minterms, as text — the chart behind MINTERMS rather than one cover of it.")

Def BlSeedTable(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "TABLE", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BlTerms("TABLE", vs, BlTableOf)), "( ms n -- s )", "The truth table over n variables that is true on exactly those minterms, as text. One string of 2^n rows.")

Rem The three share every guard and differ only in the rendering, so they
Rem share one word and take theirs as an argument.
Def BlTerms(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ List Of Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckListArg(word, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(xs)
            BlTermsOn(word, xs, Nth(vs, 2), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlTermsOn(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, c As Cell, f As [ List Of Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BlWholes(word, Append(xs, c))
        Case Ok(ns)
            BlTermsAt(word, Taken(ns, Length(ns) - 1), Nth(ns, Length(ns)), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BlTermsAt(word As String, ms As List Of Number, n As Number, f As [ List Of Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Let why = BlTermsWhy(word, ms, n)
    If why = "" Then
        RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, ms, n)) })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(why)

Rem The variable count is asked first, because 2 ^ n rows for a large n is
Rem not a slow answer but a hang, and because the minterm bound is stated
Rem in terms of it.
Def BlTermsWhy(word As String, ms As List Of Number, n As Number) As String
    If BlVarsWhy(word, n) <> "" Then
        BlVarsWhy(word, n)
    Else
        BlMintermsWhy(word, ms, n)

Def BlVarsWhy(word As String, n As Number) As String
    If n < 1 Or n > BoolMaxVars() Then
        word & " needs a variable count from 1 to " & Str(BoolMaxVars()) & ", got " & Str(n) & " — comfortable to 8, slow at 10"
    Else
        ""

Def BlMintermsWhy(word As String, ms As List Of Number, n As Number) As String
    If Any(ms, Fn(m) => m >= 2 ^ n) Then
        word & " needs every minterm from 0 to " & Str(2 ^ n - 1) & " for " & Str(n) & " variables"
    Else
        ""

Rem A, B, C ... one letter per variable. bool's rendering words take the
Rem names rather than inventing them, because a program has its own; at a
Rem keyboard there is nobody to ask and the letters are what the textbook
Rem prints. Twelve variables is the ceiling, so it never runs past L.
Def BlNames(n As Number) As List Of String
    Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Chr(64 + i))

Def BlSopOf(ms As List Of Number, n As Number) As String
    BoolSopText(BoolMinimise(ms, n), BlNames(n))

Def BlPiOf(ms As List Of Number, n As Number) As String
    BoolSopText(BoolPrimeImplicants(ms, n), BlNames(n))

Rem BoolTableText wants the FUNCTION, which a keyboard cannot supply —
Rem R6.3's exclusion, and it stands. What it can supply is the minterm
Rem list, and the predicate is built from that here, inside the seed,
Rem where a typed function value is an ordinary thing to write.
Def BlTableOf(ms As List Of Number, n As Number) As String
    BoolTableText(Fn(bs) => Contains(ms, BoolRowIndex(bs, n)), n, BlNames(n))

Rem ---- the seed -----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedBool(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let st1 = BlSeedHex(RckSeeding(st, "seed-bool"))
    Let st2 = BlSeedBin(st1)
    Let st3 = BlSeedInbase(st2)
    Let st4 = BlSeedFrombase(st3)
    Let st5 = BlSeedBitand(st4)
    Let st6 = BlSeedBitor(st5)
    Let st7 = BlSeedBitxor(st6)
    Let st8 = BlSeedBitnot(st7)
    Let st9 = BlSeedShl(st8)
    Let st10 = BlSeedShr(st9)
    Let st11 = BlSeedMask(st10)
    Let st12 = BlSeedGetfield(st11)
    Let st13 = BlSeedBitat(st12)
    Let st14 = BlSeedPopcount(st13)
    Let st15 = BlSeedNand(st14)
    Let st16 = BlSeedNor(st15)
    Let st17 = BlSeedXor(st16)
    Let st18 = BlSeedXnor(st17)
    Let st19 = BlSeedImplies(st18)
    Let st20 = BlSeedIff(st19)
    Rem Appended, in the order the families are introduced above and not
    Rem interleaved with the twenty that were here first: WORDS prints a
    Rem group in registration order, and a reader who learned the old
    Rem listing should find it unchanged with the new work after it.
    Let st21 = BlSeedRotl(st20)
    Let st22 = BlSeedRotr(st21)
    Let st23 = BlSeedShra(st22)
    Let st24 = BlSeedSigned(st23)
    Let st25 = BlSeedUnsigned(st24)
    Let st26 = BlSeedSignextend(st25)
    Let st27 = BlSeedNegw(st26)
    Let st28 = BlSeedAddw(st27)
    Let st29 = BlSeedSubw(st28)
    Let st30 = BlSeedMulw(st29)
    Let st31 = BlSeedCarries(st30)
    Let st32 = BlSeedOverflows(st31)
    Let st33 = BlSeedCard(st32)
    Let st34 = BlSeedCardwith(st33)
    Let st35 = BlSeedLowbit(st34)
    Let st36 = BlSeedHighbit(st35)
    Let st37 = BlSeedGray(st36)
    Let st38 = BlSeedFromgray(st37)
    Let st39 = BlSeedParity(st38)
    Let st40 = BlSeedParitybit(st39)
    Let st41 = BlSeedHamming(st40)
    Let st42 = BlSeedBcd(st41)
    Let st43 = BlSeedFrombcd(st42)
    Let st44 = BlSeedMinterms(st43)
    Let st45 = BlSeedImplicants(st44)
    BlSeedTable(st45)
